  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter will bring gusty winds and periods of\nrain for the first weekend of Fall \n\nBig storm will tuck itself between Long Island and NJ for the\nweekend. It will deepen to about 990mb depending on your\nnumerical model/ensemble member of choice. At least one solution\nhas the sfc low getting as far W as the Delaware River. Most\nkeep the western extent of it's wobbly track around the Jersey\nShore. \n\nThe wind already made a gust near 30KT at Wellsboro (KN38) \naround midnight and VAD Wind Profile from KCCX has 50KT winds \nnot even 4kft aloft. KPHL has just gusted to 40KT (which is wind\nadvy level). We may have to consider a wind advy, but current \nand previous thinking is that most gusts should stay in the 30s \nover our CWA. Lancaster, Lebanon, Schuylkill and Sullivan \nCounties are the most-possible locations to get a 40+KT gust.\nThe gradient stays similar in tightness from mid-day today\nthrough Sunday evening. \n\nVery light rainfall has already begun at PHL, and will work back\nto the west through the day. Expect some drops to hit the ground\nbefore 10 AM, and perhaps before 8 AM, in Lancaster Co. QPF is\nminor. The highest rainfall will be later tonight and through\nthe day Sunday. Storm total numbers range from near 1\" in New\nHolland and Dushore to less than a tenth of an inch from State \nCollege west. \n\nAs the nor'easter lifts to the northeast later Monday,\nconditions should steadily improve with temperatures poised to \nrebound above the historical average for the end of September.",
